**SSH** is three protocols stacked, as RFC 4251 describes:

- the **Transport Layer Protocol** ([RFC 4253](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4253)): server authentication, confidentiality and integrity, normally over TCP port 22;
- the **User Authentication Protocol** ([RFC 4252](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4252)): the client proves who the user is (public key, password and so on);
- the **Connection Protocol** ([RFC 4254](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4254)): many logical channels (shells, commands, port forwards) inside one encrypted connection.

This lesson is the transport layer, because that is where the next two lessons' bugs live. Choosing user keys and hardening the `sshd` server configuration are outside this course.

