**SNMPv3** adds the **USM** (User-based Security Model, [RFC 3414](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3414)): named users, each with an authentication key and a privacy (encryption) key derived from passwords. RFC 3414 lists the threats it addresses: modification of messages, **masquerade** (pretending to be another user), disclosure, and message stream modification (replay, delay, reordering). It explicitly does not address denial of service or traffic analysis.

Each message is sent at one of three **security levels**:

- `noAuthNoPriv`: no authentication, no encryption (no better than v2c);
- `authNoPriv`: authenticated with an HMAC, but readable;
- `authPriv`: authenticated and encrypted.

The original algorithms were HMAC-MD5-96 and HMAC-SHA-96 for authentication and DES for privacy. RFC 3826 added AES and [RFC 7860](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7860) added HMAC-SHA-2. Choose `authPriv` with SHA-2 and AES.

